 ],
 "literal": "Upon the young male elephant resembling a mountain / like the manner of wiping the sky / banners of diverse forms waving / O victorious king of the wide army! / The place you look with anger, fire crawls / the place you look with favour, gold blooms / even if you want moonlight from the red sun / even if you want sunlight from the white moon / since you are capable of producing what is desired / born in your shade, grown in your shade / what indeed is our measure? In this state / even in the golden-flowered groves of the good country / other than attaining according to deeds done / those who have giving and those who do not have begging / is of obligatory nature, which is helpless, they say / since the enjoyment done there is obtained here also / suppliants will think of your country / even in the lands of enemies, saying it is yours!",
 "translation": "O victorious king of the wide army, where banners of diverse forms wave, looking as if they wipe the sky, from atop young male elephants that resemble mountains! The place you look upon in anger is consumed by crawling fire; the place you look upon with favour blooms with gold. Since you have the power to bring forth whatever you desire—even if you want moonlight from the red sun or sunlight from the white moon—what is our measure, we who were born and raised under your shade? In this sweet state, they say it is a helpless thing that giving by the wealthy and begging by the poor are ordained by fate, rather than being attained solely through one's past deeds even in the golden-flowered groves of the heavenly world. Since the enjoyments of that world can be attained right here, suppliants think only of your country, even when they are in the lands of your enemies, declaring, 'This too is yours!'",
 "modern": "மலை போன்ற இளமையான ஆண் யானைகளின் மேல், வானத்தைத் துடைப்பது போலப் பல நிறக் கொடிகள் அசையும் அகன்ற படையைக் கொண்ட வெற்றி வேந்தனே! நீ கோபத்துடன் பார்க்கும் இடம் நெருப்புப் பரவிக் கெடும்; நீ அன்போடு பார்க்கும் இடம் பொன்னாகப் பொலிவுறும். செஞ்ஞாயிற்றிடம் நிலவையும், வெண்திங்களிடம் வெயிலையும் நீ விரும்பிக் கேட்டாலும், விரும்பியதை உண்டாக்கும் ஆற்றல் உனக்கு உண்டு. உனது நிழலில் பிறந்து உனது நிழலில் வளர்ந்த எங்களது நிலை என்ன? பொன் மலர்கள் நிறைந்த கற்பகச் சோலைகளை உடைய தேவருலகிலும், அவரவர் செய்த நல்வினைப் பயனை அடைவதே அல்லாமல், செல்வர் கொடுத்தலும் வறியவர் இரத்தலும் முறையன்று, அது துன்பம் தருவது என்பர். ஆனால், தேவருலகத்து இன்பங்களை இங்கேயே பெற முடிவதால், இரவலர்கள் உனது பகைவர் நாட்டில் இருந்தாலும், 'இதுவும் உன்னுடைய நாடே' என்று கூறி உனது நாட்டையே நினைப்பார்கள்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Āvūr Mūlaṅ Kiḻār praises the Chola king Kiḷḷivaḷavaṉ, who died at Kuḷamuṟṟam, in the Pāṭāṇ tiṇai (theme of praising a king's virtues and power). The poet describes the king's absolute power, stating that he can perform impossible feats, such as demanding moonlight from the sun or sunlight from the moon. He contrasts the heavenly world, where one only experiences the fruits of past deeds and cannot perform charity, with the king's realm, where the joy of giving and receiving is alive. Because the king's generosity provides heavenly pleasures right here on earth, suppliants always think of his country, even when they travel through enemy lands, viewing the entire world as belonging to him. Key words include 'ஆற்றலை' (capable one) and 'பரிசிலர்' (suppliants).",
